<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vasily Grossman</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1905">1905</a>–<a href="/years/1964">1964</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Iosif Solomonovich Grossman, Vasily Semyonovich Grossman</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Vasily Grossman was a Soviet writer and journalist, born to a Jewish family in Berdychiv in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>, then part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>, who trained as a chemical engineer before writing full-time in the 1930s. As a war correspondent for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> newspaper Krasnaya Zvezda he reported the battles of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volgograd">Stalingrad</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kursk">Kursk</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>, and his article The Hell of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treblinka-extermination-camp">Treblinka</a> (1944), among the earliest reports of a Nazi death camp, was used at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuremberg">Nuremberg</a> Trials. His novels Life and Fate and Everything Flows were suppressed as anti-Soviet, and the KGB seized the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> of Life and Fate. Smuggled abroad after his death, they were first published in the West in 1980.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vasily-grossman.jpg" alt="Vasily Grossman" width="330" height="455" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Grossman_V.S.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>He was born Iosif Solomonovich Grossman and did not receive a traditional Jewish education. His father, Semyon Osipovich Grossman, was a chemical engineer with social-democratic convictions who joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mensheviks">Mensheviks</a>; his mother, Yekaterina Savelievna, taught French. A Russian nanny turned the boy's name Yossya into Vasya, which the family accepted. He lived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a> with his mother from 1910 to 1912, moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a> in 1914, and later studied chemical engineering at Moscow State University, where his diligence earned him the nickname Vasya-khimik. He then took a job in Stalino in the Donets Basin.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Grossman began writing short stories as a student. One of the first, In the Town of Berdichev, drew encouragement from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maxim-gorky">Maxim Gorky</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mikhail-bulgakov">Mikhail Bulgakov</a>, and the film Commissar (1967), suppressed by the KGB and released only in 1988, is based on it. In the mid-1930s he left his job to write full-time, and in 1937 he was accepted into the Union of Writers. His novel Stepan Kol'chugin was nominated for a Stalin prize, but Stalin himself removed it from the list for alleged Menshevik sympathies.</p><p>In 1937, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-purge">Great Purge</a>, his wife Olga's former husband Boris Guber was arrested, and Olga was arrested for failing to denounce him. Grossman registered as guardian of her two sons and wrote to Nikolay Yezhov, head of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nkvd">NKVD</a>, arguing that she should not answer for a man she had left long before. Olga was released. In 1941 his mother was trapped in Berdychiv and murdered with 20,000 to 30,000 other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>. He volunteered for the front and spent more than 1,000 days there. In The Hell of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treblinka-extermination-camp">Treblinka</a> he gave 3 million as the number killed, far above the 750,000 to 880,000 now widely accepted, because of an error in his count of trains.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After the war the censors ordered changes to the Black Book, which Grossman had helped compile for the Jewish Anti-Fascist Committee, and in 1948 its Soviet edition was scrapped. The suppression shook his loyalty to the Soviet state. He died of kidney cancer on 14 September 1964 and was buried at the Troyekurovskoye Cemetery near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>.</p><p>Life and Fate was first published in Russian in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a> in 1980: Andrei Sakharov photographed draft pages kept by Semyon Lipkin, and Vladimir Voinovich smuggled the films abroad. It appeared in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> in 1988 under glasnost and in English in 1985. Robert Chandler's revised translation of 2006 was widely praised, and some critics have compared Grossman's novels to those of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leo-tolstoy">Leo Tolstoy</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Vasily Grossman” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The party activists who helped the secret police with the arrests and deportations were, in <b>Vasily Grossman</b>’s words, “all people who knew one another well, and knew their victims”.</blockquote><a href="/societies/soviet-union">Social Hierarchy of Stalin's Soviet Union</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Berdychiv, in what is now <a href="/countries/ukraine">Ukraine</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=49.89,28.6&amp;year=1943" aria-label="Berdychiv on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5206 427 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Vasily Grossman"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5656 697h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5656 697h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=49.89,28.6&amp;year=1943">What was here in 1943? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
